# Figma

## MCP setup

**Claude Code:** The Figma MCP server is connected at session start via the MCP config. See `docs/FIGMA_SETUP.md` for full setup.

**Cursor:** Use `/add-plugin figma` in agent chat to connect the Figma MCP server.

## MVDS Core — the design system library (CHECK THIS FIRST)

**MVDS Core is the design system for hub work.** Before drawing any component by
hand in a Figma file, check whether MVDS already has it.

| | |
|---|---|
| File | <https://www.figma.com/design/C20nU0mROzk3Zr0I9BELJF/MVDS-Core> (public view-only) |
| `fileKey` | `C20nU0mROzk3Zr0I9BELJF` |
| `libraryKey` | `lk-d54f86bcaf4bb7bd9ea1556e2af83e2ab93c839679852e05d720854cc1dedbfe8362b1503e1dbfc284ccb763a50cd240b2e52bd0af8904cf2ac7ffaaa8c09687` |
| Code | `@beckharrisdesign/mvds` — public on npmjs, repo `beckharrisdesign/mvds` |

**MVDS Core ships these 19 components** (verified against the library file
2026-09-17 — check here before concluding something is missing):

`Badge` · `Blockquote` · `Button` · `Callout` · `Card` · `Checkbox` ·
`Chrome` · `Dropzone` · `Field` · `Hero` · `Input` · `Label` · `Layer` ·
`MediaFrame` · `RadioGroupItem` · `Section` · `Select` · `Switch` ·
`Textarea`

**Search ranks MVDS below the stale kit, and multi-word queries lose it
entirely.** A query for `Select` returns MVDS Core's *fourth*, behind three
`BHD Labs / shadcn` Selects; a query for `"Select dropdown Label Card"`
returned no MVDS results at all and led an agent to conclude — wrongly, and
then to write it down — that MVDS had no Select. **Search one component name
at a time, and read `libraryName` on every row rather than taking the top
hit.**

**`includeLibraryKeys` does not work through the MCP client** (the array
parameter is rejected: *"expected array, received string"*). Until that is
fixed, scope by reading `libraryName`, or list the library's components
directly, which is authoritative:

```
use_figma(
  fileKey: "C20nU0mROzk3Zr0I9BELJF",       // MVDS Core itself
  code: `const page = figma.root.children.find((p) => p.name.trim() === "Components");
         await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page);
         return page.findAllWithCriteria({ types: ["COMPONENT_SET"] }).map((n) => n.name);`
)
```

**Never conclude a component is missing from a search result.** Confirm
against the roster above or the file itself. The cost of getting this wrong
is hand-building something the system already has — or sourcing from the kit
below.

The intended scoping call, for when the client supports it:

```
search_design_system(
  query: "Button",
  fileKey: "<the change's figma file>",
  includeLibraryKeys: ["lk-d54f86bc…09687"],   // MVDS Core only
)
```

`BHD Labs / shadcn` and `Simple Seed Organizer` are **not** the hub's design
system. Do not source components from them.

**Code is law; Figma is a generated mirror.** MVDS Core is regenerated from the
code by a one-way sync (`docs/SYNC.md` in the mvds repo, driven by authored
manifests in `figma/components/*.figma.mjs`). Component descriptions in Figma say
so: *"Edit in code, then re-run the sync — do not edit here."* Never fix an MVDS
component in Figma — fix `src/components/**` in the mvds repo and re-sync.

**Availability is per file.** A library existing is not the same as a library
being usable: `get_libraries` reports what a given file has *subscribed*, not what
exists. A **newly created** change file starts with nothing subscribed — MVDS Core
will not even appear in `libraries_available_to_add`, which lists only community
kits. It has to be turned on in the Figma UI, and only Katy can do it.

**Ask at file-creation time, not after you hit the wall.** The moment you call
`create_new_file` for a change, call `get_libraries` on the new key; if MVDS Core
is missing, give Katy this one-liner *before* doing any other Figma work, so the
subscription happens while she is already in the tool:

> Open <https://www.figma.com/design/hHAppz4A23qMoLQFTo8QAc> → **Assets** panel →
> the library icon (top right of the panel) → toggle **MVDS Core** on.
> (Replace the URL with the new file's own.)

Then re-run `get_libraries` and confirm `MVDS Core` shows with `source: "team"`
before importing anything. Do not build bespoke chrome as a workaround — the
design gate requires proposed frames built on MVDS.

**Layout primitives are deliberately absent.** `Stack`, `Inline` and `Container`
have no Figma counterparts because they carry no visual treatment — auto-layout is
their equivalent. Their absence is not a gap to fill.

## Project-specific rules

- **Map to our system** — Treat Figma MCP output as design reference, not final code. Use this project's design tokens (`tailwind.config.ts`), components (`components/`), and typography (Fraunces headings, Inter body). Validate for 1:1 visual parity with the Figma screenshot.
- **Reuse, don't duplicate** — Use existing design-system components. Do not add new icon packages if assets come from Figma. No hardcoded colors or spacing; use tokens from `tailwind.config.ts`.
- **Asset handling** — If the Figma MCP server returns a `localhost` source for an image or SVG, use that source directly. Do not create placeholders.

## OpenSpec `design.md`

For changes under `openspec/changes/<id>/`, every **`design.md` MUST include a “Visual design / Figma” subsection** (use “N/A” table rows for API-only work). For UI work, specify:

- Primary **Figma file URL** (`figma.com/design/...` or board URL)
- **Published libraries** used — normally `MVDS Core` (see above). If a change uses anything else, say which and why
- **Frames/components in scope**
- Whether **Code Connect** mappings need updates (`figma-code-connect` skill)

### When the first round happens

**A UI change gets its first Figma round before or alongside the proposal**,
not at `design.md`. This applies to changes created **after 2026-09-17**;
anything already in flight keeps the sequence it was built on, and its
`02 Proposed` may legitimately be empty because the first iteration belonged
to `design.md`. Do not renumber an existing change to match. Scope written blind is scope you re-author after every
drawing; the round is what makes the proposal arguable. Keep it rough — shape
and composition. The detailed as-is + proposed pair, breakpoints and the Visual
design table still belong to `design.md`, which **continues** the numbering
rather than starting it.

**Which editor.** Pick by the question the change is asking, not by fidelity.

- A change about a **flow, sequence, structure or set of relationships** gets a
  **FigJam board** (`figma.com/board/...`). A board cannot produce something
  polished enough to be mistaken for an approval-ready design — the constraint is
  the point this early — and it subscribes no libraries, so there is no MVDS step
  before you can think.
- A change about a **surface** — a panel, page, component or layout — gets a rough
  frame in a **design file** (`figma.com/design/...`). "What does this look like
  once X is gone" is not a question a board can answer.

Some changes need both. When they do, the numbering runs continuously **across**
the files and the `design.md` Visual design table names each one and says which
rounds live where.

### File page/frame naming convention (REQUIRED)

Every OpenSpec Figma file uses **numbered pages** and treats each proposal iteration as a distinct page — never edit or append to an existing proposal page in place:

- `00 Components` — imported DS components + local "hub tokens" variable collection.
- `01 Current state` — the as-is frame(s), reconstructed from code or captured from the running app.
- `02 Proposed` — the first proposed iteration.
- `02.1`, `02.2`, … — **each subsequent proposal iteration is a NEW numbered page** (`02.1 Proposed — <what changed>`), so prior iterations stay intact for comparison. **Never** overwrite or append frames on an already-built/approved proposal page.
- Name frames within a page by treatment + breakpoint, e.g. `Proposed · Desktop 1024`, `Proposed · Mobile 480`.
- **FigJam boards have no pages** — a board is one canvas. Use **named sections** in
  place of numbered pages (`01 Current state`, `02 Proposed`, `02.1 Proposed — <what
  changed>`), one section per iteration. The rule is unchanged: never overwrite or
  append to a round that has already been built.

Record the exact page names + frame node IDs in the `design.md` Visual design table, and restate this file convention there (as `stop-the-leaks/design.md` does).

Map kit variables to **`tailwind.config.ts`** tokens; treat MCP-exported layout as reference, not literals (see Project-specific rules above).

